---
import StudioLayout from '../../layouts/StudioLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/thermometry.css';
import '../../styles/kinetic-theory.css';
---
<StudioLayout title="Kinetic Theory of Gases - Physics with Harshith" chapterChip="Physics · Kinetic Theory">

  <!-- =========== HOME =========== -->
  <section id="home" class="screen active">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>Kinetic Theory <span class="accent">of Gases</span></h1>
      <p>A gas is just a huge number of tiny molecules flying around and bouncing off the walls. Every one of those bounces pushes on the wall - add them all up and you get pressure. Follow that one idea all the way to temperature and molecular speed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">

      <div class="tile" onclick="location.href='/kinetic-theory/pressure-of-an-ideal-gas'">
        <div class="tile-num">01</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-gas"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><circle cx="8" cy="9" r="1.5"/><circle cx="15" cy="7" r="1.5"/><circle cx="11" cy="15" r="1.5"/><path d="M16.5 13.5l3 1.5M9.5 9.5l2-1"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Pressure of an Ideal Gas</h3>
        <p>Slides. One molecule in a cube, one collision, then all N of them - pressure from momentum, then kinetic energy, temperature, v<sub>rms</sub> and the Maxwell-Boltzmann speed distribution.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Open slides &rsaquo;</span>
      </div>

      <a class="tile" href="/downloads/kinetic-theory/gas-processes-and-their-graphs.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <div class="tile-num">02</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-graphs"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M6 6c1 7 5 11 14 12"/><path d="M6 15h13"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Gas Processes &amp; Their Graphs</h3>
        <p>Notes PDF. Isothermal, isobaric and isochoric on P-V, P-T and V-T graphs, three rules to redraw any cycle, and solved cycle problems.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Open PDF &rsaquo;</span>
      </a>

      <div class="tile" onclick="location.href='/kinetic-theory/walls-pistons-and-degrees-of-freedom'">
        <div class="tile-num">03</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-dof"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 4v16h14V4"/><path d="M5 10h14"/><path d="M12 10V2"/><circle cx="9" cy="15.5" r="1"/><circle cx="15" cy="14.5" r="1"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Walls, Pistons and Degrees of Freedom</h3>
        <p>Slides. Adiabatic and conducting walls, the diathermic separator and what a fixed or movable piston does to T, P and V - then degrees of freedom for monatomic, diatomic and triatomic gases.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Open slides &rsaquo;</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('piston')">
        <div class="tile-num">04</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-piston"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="2" y="5" width="20" height="14" rx="1.5"/><path d="M12 5v14"/><path d="M5 12h4M7.5 10l1.5 2-1.5 2"/><path d="M19 12h-4M16.5 10L15 12l1.5 2"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Adiabatic Walls &amp; a Piston</h3>
        <p>Animation. Two gases in a box no heat can leave. Pick the piston - conducting or not, fixed or free - release it and watch which of T, P and V end up equal.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start &rsaquo;</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="location.href='/kinetic-theory/equipartition-manometer-and-barometer'">
        <div class="tile-num">05</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-mano"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M7 3v11a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V5"/><path d="M4 12h6M14 8h6"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Equipartition, Manometer and Barometer</h3>
        <p>Slides. Each degree of freedom gets &frac12;kT, so U = (f/2)nRT - then the manometer, the barometer and trapped-gas problems with mercury columns and pellets.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Open slides &rsaquo;</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- =========== 4 · ADIABATIC WALLS AND A PISTON =========== -->
  <section id="piston" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Adiabatic Walls &amp; a Piston</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">Two gases in a box that no heat can enter or leave. Pick the piston, press Start, and see what ends up equal.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-btns" id="ktCase">
        <button class="th-btn on" data-case="0">Non-conducting, fixed</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-case="1">Non-conducting, movable</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-case="2">Conducting, fixed</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-case="3">Conducting, movable</button>
      </div>
      <div class="th-btns">
        <button class="th-btn primary" id="ktGo">▶ Start</button>
        <button class="th-btn" id="ktReset">↺ Reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Gas 1 temperature T₁ <span class="th-val" id="ktT1v">800 K</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="ktT1" min="200" max="800" step="50" value="800">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Gas 1 amount n₁ <span class="th-val" id="ktN1v">1 mol</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="ktN1" min="1" max="3" step="1" value="1">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Gas 2 temperature T₂ <span class="th-val" id="ktT2v">200 K</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="ktT2" min="200" max="800" step="50" value="200">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Gas 2 amount n₂ <span class="th-val" id="ktN2v">2 mol</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="ktN2" min="1" max="3" step="1" value="2">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-canvas" id="ktPistonCanvas"></div>

    <div class="th-readouts">
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Temperature gap</small><div class="th-ro" id="ktRoT">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Pressure gap</small><div class="th-ro" id="ktRoP">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Volumes V₁ : V₂</small><div class="th-ro small" id="ktRoV">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout kt-wide"><small>What is happening</small><div class="th-ro small" id="ktRoS">-</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="kt-table-wrap">
      <table class="kt-table" id="ktTable">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Piston</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Pressure</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr data-case="0" class="cur">
            <th>Non-conducting, fixed</th>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a same">no change</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a same">no change</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a same">no change</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr data-case="1">
            <th>Non-conducting, movable</th>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a chg">T₁, T₂ change</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a eq">P₁ = P₂ finally</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a chg">V₁, V₂ change</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr data-case="2">
            <th>Conducting, fixed</th>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a eq">T₁ = T₂ finally</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a chg">P₁, P₂ change</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a same">no change</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr data-case="3">
            <th>Conducting, movable</th>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a eq">T₁ = T₂ finally</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a eq">P₁ = P₂ finally</span></td>
            <td><span class="kt-q">?</span><span class="kt-a chg">V₁, V₂ change</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="kt-tablebar">
      <p>Tap a row to load that piston. Its answers fill in once the run settles.</p>
      <button class="th-btn" id="ktReveal">Reveal all answers</button>
    </div>

    <div class="kt-rules">
      <div class="kt-rule cond" id="ktRuleCond"><b>Conducting</b> piston → temperatures become equal.</div>
      <div class="kt-rule mov" id="ktRuleMov"><b>Movable</b> piston → pressures become equal.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-keybox">
      <b>Colour is temperature: blue is cold, purple is warm, red is hot.</b> The arrows on the piston are the pushes P₁A and P₂A. A fixed piston is bolted, so unequal pushes do nothing. A free piston slides towards the weaker push until the two pushes match - and the gas that expands does work, so it cools, while the squeezed gas warms up. A conducting piston lets heat leak from hot to cold until the two colours match. Watch the three graphs: lines that meet are quantities that become equal.
    </div>

    <div class="th-notes">
      <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
      <div class="th-notes-body">
        <h4>Walls, separators and pistons</h4>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          <b>Adiabatic (non-conducting) wall:</b> no heat exchange between the gas and the surroundings.<br>
          <b>Conducting wall:</b> heat flows until T(gas) = T(surroundings).<br>
          <b>Diathermic separator:</b> lets heat through too, but much more slowly than a conducting wall.
        </div>
        <p><b>Conducting piston</b> → heat can cross, so the temperatures become equal.</p>
        <p><b>Movable piston</b> → it slides until the pushes balance, so the pressures become equal.</p>
        <p><b>Fixed piston</b> → it cannot move, so the volumes cannot change.</p>
        <h4>Conducting and movable: how the volume is shared</h4>
        <p>Finally P₁ = P₂ and T₁ = T₂ = T. Use PV = nRT on each side:</p>
        <div class="th-nf">n₁RT / V₁ = n₂RT / V₂ &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; V₁ / n₁ = V₂ / n₂</div>
        <p>The volume is shared in the ratio of the number of moles - it does not matter what the starting temperatures were.</p>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Think about it</span>
          Run "Conducting, fixed" with the starting values. Gas 1 starts at the higher pressure. Which gas has the higher pressure at the end, and why?
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            Gas 2. Once the temperatures are equal, P = nRT/V depends only on n/V. The volumes are equal and gas 2 has twice the moles, so it ends at twice the pressure of gas 1. Hot gas 1 was winning only because it was hotter.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Think about it</span>
          With a non-conducting, movable piston, no heat can reach either gas. So why does gas 1 cool down and gas 2 warm up?
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            Work. Gas 1 pushes the piston and does work on it, and with no heat coming in to make up for it, its molecules slow down - it cools. Gas 2 gets squeezed, so work is done on it and its molecules speed up - it warms. The total energy of the two gases stays the same.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</StudioLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/kinetic-theory-studio';
</script>
